<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="es">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0, maximum-scale=1.0">
<title>Tarjetas del Juego</title>
<style>
  @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Cormorant+Garamond:ital,wght@0,400;0,600;0,700;1,400;1,600&family=DM+Sans:wght@300;400;500;600&display=swap');

  :root {
    --gold: #C9A624;
    --gold-light: #E8D070;
    --gold-pale: #F5E9C0;
    --teal: #2AABB8;
    --teal-dark: #1e8a96;
    --teal-deeper: #155f69;
    --teal-pale: #e0f5f7;
    --black: #1A1A1A;
    --cream: #FDFCF6;
    --white: #ffffff;
    --grey: #5a9aa3;
    --green: #2AABB8;
    --green-light: #50c5d0;
    --card-w: min(380px, 92vw);
    --card-h: min(560px, 78vh);
  }

  * { box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0; padding: 0; }

  body {
    background: #ffffff;
    font-family: 'DM Sans', sans-serif;
    min-height: 100vh;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    overflow-x: hidden;
    color: var(--black);
  }

  /* ── Top bar ── */
  .topbar {
    width: 100%;
    background: var(--teal);
    border-bottom: 3px solid var(--gold);
    padding: 14px 20px;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    z-index: 100;
  }
  .logo {
    font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif;
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--gold);
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  }
  .logo span { color: #fff; font-weight: 400; }

  /* ── Deck selector ── */
  .deck-section {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 520px;
    padding: 20px 16px 10px;
  }
  .deck-section h2 {
    font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif;
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--teal-dark);
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 12px;
  }
  .deck-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: 8px;
  }
  .deck-btn {
    background: var(--teal-pale);
    border: 1.5px solid var(--teal);
    color: var(--teal-deeper);
    padding: 10px 12px;
    border-radius: 8px;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-family: 'DM Sans', sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    text-align: left;
    transition: all 0.18s;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }
  .deck-btn:hover { border-color: var(--gold); background: #fff8dc; }
  .deck-btn.active {
    background: var(--teal);
    border-color: var(--gold);
    color: #fff;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .deck-btn .icon { font-size: 1.1rem; flex-shrink: 0; }
  .deck-btn .count {
    margin-left: auto;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    opacity: 0.6;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }
  .deck-btn.gold-node {
    border-color: var(--gold);
    background: #fff8dc;
    color: #7a5a00;
  }
  .deck-btn.gold-node.active {
    background: var(--gold);
    border-color: var(--gold);
    color: var(--black);
  }
  .deck-btn.labour {
    border-color: var(--teal-dark);
    background: #d4f0f3;
    color: var(--teal-deeper);
  }
  .deck-btn.labour.active {
    background: var(--teal-dark);
    border-color: var(--teal-dark);
    color: #fff;
  }

  /* ── Card stage ── */
  .stage {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 520px;
    padding: 16px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 16px;
    flex: 1;
  }

  .deck-label {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--teal-dark);
    align-self: flex-start;
  }
  .deck-label span { color: var(--teal-deeper); font-weight: 600; }

  /* ── Card ── */
  .card-wrap {
    width: var(--card-w);
    height: var(--card-h);
    perspective: 1200px;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .card-inner {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    position: relative;
    transform-style: preserve-3d;
    transition: transform 0.55s cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1);
  }
  .card-inner.flipped { transform: rotateY(180deg); }

  .card-face {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    border-radius: 18px;
    backface-visibility: hidden;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* Front */
  .card-front {
    background: var(--teal);
    border: 2.5px solid var(--gold);
  }
  .card-front.gold-node {
    background: linear-gradient(160deg, #2AABB8, #1e8a96);
    border-color: var(--gold);
    border-width: 3px;
  }
  .card-front.labour-card {
    background: linear-gradient(160deg, #1e8a96, #155f69);
    border-color: var(--gold);
    border-width: 2.5px;
  }

  .card-header {
    padding: 14px 18px 10px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.2);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
  }
  .card-type {
    font-family: 'DM Sans', sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #fff;
  }
  .card-badge {
    font-size: 0.65rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    padding: 3px 8px;
    border-radius: 20px;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  }
  .badge-easy { background: rgba(255,255,255,0.25); color: #fff; }
  .badge-medium { background: var(--gold); color: var(--black); }
  .badge-hard { background: #1A1A1A; color: #fff; }
  .badge-gold { background: var(--gold); color: var(--black); }
  .badge-green { background: rgba(255,255,255,0.2); color: #fff; }

  .card-body {
    flex: 1;
    padding: 18px 20px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 10px;
  }
  .card-theme {
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: rgba(255,255,255,0.7);
  }
  .card-question {
    font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif;
    font-size: 1.3rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: #fff;
  }
  .card-options {
    margin-top: 6px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 5px;
  }
  .card-option {
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: #fff;
    padding: 6px 10px;
    background: rgba(255,255,255,0.15);
    border-radius: 6px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--gold);
  }
  .card-tap-hint {
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    color: rgba(255,255,255,0.6);
    text-align: center;
    margin-top: auto;
    padding-top: 8px;
  }
  .tap-star { color: var(--gold); }

  .card-footer {
    padding: 8px 18px 12px;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.15);
    font-size: 0.65rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: rgba(255,255,255,0.5);
    text-align: center;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  /* Back */
  .card-back {
    background: var(--teal-deeper);
    border: 2.5px solid var(--gold);
    transform: rotateY(180deg);
  }
  .card-back.gold-node { border-color: var(--gold); border-width: 3px; }
  .card-back.labour-card { border-color: var(--gold); }

  .back-header {
    padding: 14px 18px 10px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.15);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
  }
  .back-header-label {
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--gold);
  }

  .back-body {
    flex: 1;
    padding: 16px 20px;
    overflow-y: auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 12px;
  }
  .answer-label {
    font-size: 0.65rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--gold);
  }
  .answer-text {
    font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif;
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: #fff;
    font-weight: 400;
  }
  .facilitator-box {
    background: rgba(255,255,255,0.08);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.2);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 10px 14px;
  }
  .facilitator-label {
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: rgba(255,255,255,0.6);
    margin-bottom: 4px;
  }
  .facilitator-text {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: rgba(255,255,255,0.8);
  }
  .mechanic-box {
    background: rgba(201,166,36,0.15);
    border: 1px solid var(--gold);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 10px 14px;
  }
  .mechanic-label {
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--gold);
    margin-bottom: 4px;
  }
  .mechanic-text {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: rgba(255,255,255,0.85);
  }
  .token-reward {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 6px;
    background: var(--gold);
    color: var(--black);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    padding: 5px 12px;
    border-radius: 20px;
    align-self: flex-start;
  }

  /* ── Controls ── */
  .controls {
    display: flex;
    gap: 10px;
    width: var(--card-w);
  }
  .btn {
    flex: 1;
    padding: 13px;
    border-radius: 10px;
    border: none;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-family: 'DM Sans', sans-serif;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    transition: all 0.18s;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
  }
  .btn-draw {
    background: var(--teal);
    color: #fff;
    border: 2px solid var(--gold);
  }
  .btn-draw:hover { background: var(--teal-dark); }
  .btn-draw:active { transform: scale(0.97); }
  .btn-reveal {
    background: #fff;
    color: var(--teal-deeper);
    border: 1.5px solid var(--teal);
  }
  .btn-shuffle {
    flex: 0 0 48px;
    background: #fff;
    color: var(--teal-deeper);
    border: 1.5px solid var(--teal);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    padding: 13px 0;
  }
  .btn-shuffle:hover { border-color: var(--gold); transform: rotate(180deg); }
  .btn-shuffle:active { transform: scale(0.95) rotate(180deg); }

  .card-counter {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    color: var(--teal-dark);
    text-align: center;
  }
  .card-counter span { color: var(--teal-deeper); font-weight: 600; }

  /* ── Empty state ── */
  .empty-state {
    width: var(--card-w);
    height: var(--card-h);
    border: 2px dashed var(--teal);
    border-radius: 18px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 12px;
    color: var(--teal-dark);
    background: var(--teal-pale);
  }
  .empty-state .icon { font-size: 2.5rem; opacity: 0.5; }
  .empty-state p { font-size: 0.85rem; text-align: center; max-width: 200px; line-height: 1.5; }

  /* ── Slide animation ── */
  @keyframes slideIn {
    from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(20px) scale(0.96); }
    to   { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0) scale(1); }
  }
  .card-wrap { animation: slideIn 0.3s ease forwards; }

  /* ── Gold shimmer on gold node cards ── */
  .gold-shimmer {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0; left: -100%;
    width: 60%;
    height: 100%;
    background: linear-gradient(90deg, transparent, rgba(201,166,36,0.15), transparent);
    animation: shimmer 2.5s infinite;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  @keyframes shimmer {
    0% { left: -100%; }
    100% { left: 200%; }
  }

  /* ── Labour Loop square header ── */
  .labour-square-name {
    font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--green-light);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  }

  /* Scrollbar */
  .back-body::-webkit-scrollbar { width: 4px; }
  .back-body::-webkit-scrollbar-track { background: transparent; }
  .back-body::-webkit-scrollbar-thumb { background: #444; border-radius: 2px; }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="topbar">
  <div class="logo">BIRTH LOOP <span>Tarjetas del Juego</span></div>
  <div style="font-size:0.7rem;color:rgba(255,255,255,0.7)">Toca la tarjeta para revelar</div>
</div>

<div class="deck-section">
  <h2>Choose a deck</h2>
  <div class="deck-grid" id="deckGrid"></div>
</div>

<div class="stage">
  <div class="deck-label" id="deckLabel">Select a deck above</div>
  <div id="cardStage">
    <div class="empty-state">
      <div class="icon">🃏</div>
      <p>Selecciona un mazo y toca Sacar Tarjeta</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="controls" id="controls" style="display:none">
    <button class="btn btn-reveal" id="btnReveal" onclick="flipCard()">Ver Respuesta</button>
    <button class="btn btn-draw" id="btnDraw" onclick="drawCard()">Sacar Tarjeta</button>
    <button class="btn btn-shuffle" onclick="reshuffleDeck()" title="Barajar mazo">🔀</button>
  </div>
  <div class="card-counter" id="counter"></div>
</div>

<script>
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// CARD DATA
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
const DECKS = {

  // ── KNOWLEDGE ─────────────────────────────────────
  knowledge: {
    label: 'Conocimiento', icon: '📚', count: 54, type: 'circuit',
    cards: [
      { format:'Health Literacy', diff:'Easy', q:'Your notes say "fundal height 34cm at 36 weeks, plotting below 10th centile, referred for growth scan." What does this mean?', a:'Your bump measurement is smaller than expected for this stage. A scan is being arranged to check the baby\'s size more accurately. It does not mean something is wrong.', fac:'Key message: a referral is precaution, not diagnosis. Reinforce the team is being thorough.' },
      { format:'Health Literacy', diff:'Medium', q:'Your discharge letter says: "Para 1+0, SVD at 39+4, blood loss 350ml, Apgar 9 at 1 min." Decode this in plain language.', a:'Your first baby (+0 previous losses). Straightforward vaginal birth at 39 weeks 4 days. Blood loss 350ml (normal is under 500ml). Baby scored 9/10 on health check at 1 minute old.', fac:'Breaking down discharge letters builds confidence for future appointments.' },
      { format:'Health Literacy', diff:'Medium', q:'A consent form says: "Episiotomy may be performed if clinically indicated." What are you agreeing to?', a:'Pre-consenting to a small surgical cut to the perineum during birth if the team judges it necessary. You can ask: under what circumstances would this happen?', fac:'Key: "may" and "if indicated" mean it is not guaranteed. Ask for the criteria beforehand.' },
      { format:'Health Literacy', diff:'Hard', q:'Your blood results show Hb 98 g/L. Normal range is 110–160 g/L. What does this mean and what will likely be recommended?', a:'Your haemoglobin (iron) is low — you have anaemia. In pregnancy this can cause tiredness and affect the baby\'s oxygen supply. Iron supplements or dietary changes are likely.', fac:'Ask: what foods are high in iron? Reinforce that anaemia in pregnancy is common and treatable.' },
      { format:'Quiz', diff:'Easy', q:'What does EDD stand for on your maternity notes — and is it exact?', a:'Estimated Due Date. Not exact — only around 5% of babies are born on their EDD. It marks the midpoint of a normal range: 37–42 weeks is considered term.', fac:'Reduces anxiety about going overdue. The window is normal.' },
      { format:'Quiz', diff:'Easy', q:'What does it mean when your waters break and the fluid is green or brown?', a:'Green or brown amniotic fluid means the baby has passed meconium (first stool) in the womb. Call your maternity unit immediately — do not wait. It can indicate fetal distress.', fac:'Reinforce: any colour other than clear or pale straw = call triage now.' },
      { format:'Taboo', diff:'Medium', q:'Describe PLACENTA without using: organ, baby, nutrients, oxygen, or womb.', a:'No single answer. Good responses: the life-support system grown during pregnancy, the connection between mother and child that feeds and protects.', fac:'Celebrate creative descriptions. Builds confidence naming body parts without clinical anxiety.' },
      { format:'Quiz', diff:'Medium', q:'What is the difference between an epidural and a spinal block?', a:'An epidural delivers continuous pain relief via a catheter and can be topped up. A spinal block is a single injection that acts faster, typically used for caesareans. A combined spinal-epidural (CSE) uses both.', fac:'Demystifying the procedure reduces fear.' },
      { format:'Dingbats', diff:'Easy', q:'🏠 + ✉️ + 🤰 = ?', a:'Post Partum Haemorrhage — heavy bleeding after birth.', fac:'Key awareness card. Normal blood loss after birth is under 500ml.' },
      { format:'Quiz', diff:'Medium', q:"What is Martha's Law and does it matter for pregnant women?", a:"Martha's Law gives patients and families the right to request an urgent review from a senior clinical team if they are worried about a deterioration in their or their baby's condition that isn't being acted on.", fac:"You have the right to escalate. Always." },
      { format:'True/False', diff:'Easy', q:'TRUE or FALSE: You are legally entitled to a copy of your full maternity notes.', a:'TRUE. Under the Data Protection Act 2018 (UK GDPR), you can request your maternity records at any time. The NHS must respond within one month.', fac:'Tip: request notes during pregnancy, not just after.' },
      { format:'True/False', diff:'Hard', q:'TRUE or FALSE: If you decline a clinical recommendation during labour, the maternity team can legally withdraw your care.', a:'FALSE. The duty of care continues regardless of whether you accept recommendations. Declining one intervention does not mean other support is withdrawn.', fac:'This fear is sometimes used to pressure patients. Name it directly and challenge it.' },
      { format:'True/False', diff:'Medium', q:'TRUE or FALSE: You can write a birth plan that includes your wishes in the event of a caesarean section.', a:'TRUE. A caesarean birth plan can include: who is present, music, lowered screen for the birth moment, delayed cord clamping, skin-to-skin in theatre, immediate breastfeeding.', fac:'Caesarean birth plans are underused. Encourage all women to plan for multiple birth scenarios.' },
      { format:'True/False', diff:'Easy', q:'TRUE or FALSE: Once you are in active labour, you can no longer change your mind about your birth preferences.', a:'FALSE. You retain full decision-making rights at every stage of labour, including the right to change your mind in any direction.', fac:'Common myth. Reinforce that capacity — not pain or progress — determines decision-making rights.' },
      { format:'Quiz', diff:'Hard', q:'The Birthrights report found certain groups were disproportionately referred to police or social services during pregnancy. Name two factors associated with this.', a:'Any two: being from an ethnic minority background, mental health history, substance use, previous social services involvement, poverty, being a young mother.', fac:'Handle with care. This is about systemic inequality, not individual blame.' },
      { format:'Scenario', diff:'Medium', q:'You are being asked to consent to a vaginal examination but you don\'t want one right now. What do you say?\nA) Say nothing and let it happen\nB) "I\'d like to wait — can we check again in an hour?"\nC) "Fine, get it over with"', a:'B — You can decline or delay any examination. Asking for a pause is assertive and appropriate. VEs are not always clinically necessary when offered.', fac:'Reinforce: "no" to a vaginal examination is always valid. No justification required.', options:['A) Say nothing and let it happen', 'B) "I\'d like to wait — can we check again in an hour?"', 'C) "Fine, get it over with"'] },
      { format:'Dingbats', diff:'Medium', q:'📄 + ✍️ + ❓ = ?', a:'Informed Consent — reading, questioning, and only signing when you fully understand what you are agreeing to.', fac:'Ask: what makes people sign things they don\'t understand in hospital?' },
      { format:'Taboo', diff:'Easy', q:'Describe BIRTH PLAN without using: paper, preferences, wishes, plan, or write.', a:'No single answer. Good responses: your voice before labour starts, a document of your choices, communication with your care team about what matters to you.', fac:'Celebrate any answer that centres the woman\'s agency rather than the document itself.' },
      { format:'Scenario', diff:'Easy', q:'Your midwife recommends a membrane sweep at 40 weeks. Which response uses BRAIN correctly?\nA) "OK if you think it\'s best"\nB) "What are the benefits, risks and alternatives? What if I wait?"\nC) "No thanks"', a:'B — BRAIN: Benefits, Risks, Alternatives, Intuition, Nothing (do nothing). A is passive, C skips inquiry entirely.', fac:'Ask players to role-play option B with the person next to them.', options:['A) "OK if you think it\'s best"','B) "What are the benefits, risks and alternatives? What if I wait?"','C) "No thanks"'] },
      { format:'Scenario', diff:'Hard', q:'Your consultant recommends induction at 38 weeks as your baby is measuring large on a scan. What is the most important question?\nA) "How big is too big?"\nB) "How accurate is the scan and what are my options if I wait?"\nC) "Will it hurt?"', a:'B — Scan measurements at late gestation can be ±20% inaccurate. Questioning accuracy and alternatives is the most informed starting point.', fac:'Bukky can share real figures on scan accuracy if in the room.', options:['A) "How big is too big?"','B) "How accurate is the scan and what are my options if I wait?"','C) "Will it hurt?"'] },
      { format:'Scenario', diff:'Easy', q:'You arrive at triage with reduced fetal movements. You\'ve waited 2 hours and haven\'t been seen. What do you do?\nA) Go home and return tomorrow\nB) Wait quietly — they\'re busy\nC) Speak to staff and explain your concern hasn\'t been assessed', a:'C — Reduced fetal movement is always a priority. You should be assessed within 1 hour. It is appropriate and safe to advocate for yourself.', fac:'Reinforce: you are never being difficult by asking about reduced movement.', options:['A) Go home and return tomorrow','B) Wait quietly — they\'re busy','C) Speak to staff and explain your concern hasn\'t been assessed'] },
      { format:'Scenario', diff:'Medium', q:'Your birth plan says avoid epidural unless you ask. In active labour a midwife offers one. Best response?\nA) Accept — you\'re in pain\nB) "That\'s in my birth plan — I\'ll ask if I want it. Can you support me with other pain relief now?"\nC) Say nothing and feel guilty', a:'B — Your birth plan is your voice. Calmly referencing it and asking for alternatives is assertive and appropriate.', fac:'Discuss: what makes it hard to say no when you\'re in pain?', options:['A) Accept — you\'re in pain','B) "That\'s in my birth plan — I\'ll ask if I want it."','C) Say nothing and feel guilty'] },
      { format:'Scenario', diff:'Hard', q:'You are being pressured to accept continuous fetal monitoring but want to stay mobile. Strongest response?\nA) "Fine, I\'ll lie down"\nB) "Is there evidence CTG is better than intermittent monitoring in my situation? Can I see it?"\nC) "I\'ll do whatever you think is safest"', a:'B — For low-risk labours, intermittent monitoring has comparable outcomes to CTG. Asking for evidence is your right.', fac:'Bukky can explain the NICE guideline difference between low and high-risk monitoring.', options:['A) "Fine, I\'ll lie down"','B) "Is there evidence CTG is better? Can I see it?"','C) "I\'ll do whatever you think is safest"'] },
      { format:'Scenario', diff:'Easy', q:'After a 20-week scan you\'re told there\'s a "minor finding" but "try not to worry". What do you do?\nA) Trust them and go home\nB) Ask: "Can you tell me exactly what was found and what it means?"\nC) Google it immediately', a:'B — You are entitled to a clear explanation. "Try not to worry" is not clinical information. Asking directly is safe and appropriate.', fac:'Minor finding language is common. Empower women to ask for specifics without feeling rude.', options:['A) Trust them and go home','B) Ask for a clear explanation','C) Google it immediately'] },
      { format:'True/False', diff:'Medium', q:'TRUE or FALSE: Once an induction of labour has started, you cannot change your mind and ask for it to stop.', a:'FALSE. You can withdraw consent at any stage of an induction. The process can be paused or stopped if you have capacity and request it.', fac:'Reinforce that induction starting does not mean rights end.' },
      { format:'Taboo', diff:'Medium', q:'Describe INFORMED CONSENT without using: permission, agree, sign, form, or allow.', a:'No single answer. Good responses focus on: understanding, choice, knowledge, freedom, decision based on full information.', fac:'Group votes. Listen for language that centres the patient rather than the system.' },
      { format:'Dingbats', diff:'Medium', q:'🤔 + ⏸️ + 🗺️ = ?', a:'Pause and Plan — taking a moment before making a decision to think through your options rather than reacting under pressure.', fac:'Award bonus token if a player names a real situation where pausing before deciding would matter.' },
      { format:'Quiz', diff:'Easy', q:'What are the three stages of labour?', a:'Stage 1: Cervix dilates from 0–10cm (latent and active phases). Stage 2: Pushing and birth of the baby. Stage 3: Delivery of the placenta.', fac:'Ask: which stage do most people imagine? Usually Stage 2 — opens discussion about the full journey.' },
      { format:'Quiz', diff:'Medium', q:'Name three positions that can help progress in labour.', a:'Any three: upright standing, walking, hands and knees, kneeling, sitting on a birth ball, squatting, side-lying, lunging.', fac:'Ask players to demonstrate one if comfortable. Movement in labour is evidence-based and often underused.' },
      { format:'True/False', diff:'Easy', q:'TRUE or FALSE: The latent phase of labour (early labour) can last several days.', a:'TRUE. The latent phase can last 24–72 hours, especially in a first pregnancy. Contractions may be irregular and the cervix may dilate slowly.', fac:'Many women feel they are failing if early labour is long. Normalise it.' },
      { format:'True/False', diff:'Medium', q:'TRUE or FALSE: Skin-to-skin contact after birth should only happen after the baby has been weighed and checked.', a:'FALSE. Skin-to-skin should happen immediately after birth, before weighing and routine checks (unless there is a clinical emergency). It regulates temperature, heart rate and supports feeding.', fac:'Reinforce: weighing can wait. Skin-to-skin in the first hour matters enormously.' },
      { format:'Quiz', diff:'Easy', q:"What does it mean if your baby is in the 'occiput posterior' position?", a:"The baby is head-down but with the back of their head facing your spine rather than your abdomen — known as back-to-back. This can make labour longer and more uncomfortable but birth is still possible.", fac:'Contrast with occiput anterior (ideal position).' },
      { format:'Quiz', diff:'Hard', q:'What is the purpose of delayed cord clamping and when is it recommended?', a:'Waiting at least 1 minute (ideally 3+) before cutting the umbilical cord allows the baby to receive iron-rich blood from the placenta. NICE recommends it for all births unless there is a clinical emergency.', fac:'Ask: is delayed cord clamping in your birth plan?' },
      { format:'Taboo', diff:'Medium', q:'Describe CONTRACTION without using: pain, squeeze, tighten, wave, or labour.', a:'No single answer. Good responses: a muscular surge, the womb working to open the cervix, a powerful rhythmic movement, pressure that builds and releases.', fac:'Reframing contractions as "surges" is used in hypnobirthing — worth naming if it comes up.' },
      { format:'Scenario', diff:'Medium', q:'You are 39 weeks and your baby has been moving less than usual today. What do you do?\nA) Eat something sweet and lie on your left side for an hour\nB) Call your maternity unit now\nC) Wait until tomorrow\'s appointment', a:'B — Any reduction in fetal movement requires immediate contact with your maternity unit, any time of day or night. Do not wait.', fac:'RCOG and NHS guidance is clear: reduced movement = call now. No home tests replace clinical assessment.', options:['A) Eat something sweet and lie down','B) Call your maternity unit now','C) Wait until tomorrow\'s appointment'] },
      { format:'Dingbats', diff:'Easy', q:'💨 + 😮‍💨 + 🔢 = ?', a:'Breathing Techniques — using counted, rhythmic breathing patterns to manage contractions and stay calm during labour.', fac:'Practise one breathing pattern together: 4 counts in, hold for 2, 6 counts out.' },
      { format:'Advocacy', diff:'Easy', q:"Your midwife dismisses a concern by saying 'that's completely normal, don't worry.' You're not reassured. Say out loud what you would say next.", a:"Good responses: 'I understand it may be common, but I'd like to understand why it's happening.' / 'Can you explain what's causing it so I feel more informed?'", fac:'The goal is not to argue — it\'s to stay in the conversation and get real information.' },
      { format:'Advocacy', diff:'Medium', q:'You are the only Black woman in the antenatal clinic. The midwife calls a name — not yours — then looks directly at you. How do you respond?\nA) Stand up anyway\nB) Stay seated and say: "I think you might be looking for someone else — my name is [name]"\nC) Say nothing', a:'B — Calmly correcting a mistaken assumption without shame is self-advocacy. You are not being difficult; you are being accurate.', fac:'Open discussion: how often does this happen? What does it feel like?', options:['A) Stand up anyway','B) Calmly correct the assumption','C) Say nothing'] },
      { format:'Advocacy', diff:'Hard', q:'After your birth you feel something went wrong but no one has explained it. You want answers. Say out loud how you would begin that conversation.', a:'Good responses: "I\'d like a postnatal debrief to discuss what happened." / "Can I speak to the consultant who was on call?" / "I\'d like to see my notes and have them explained to me."', fac:'Reinforce: you are entitled to a birth debrief. It is not a complaint — it is your right.' },
      { format:'Quiz', diff:'Easy', q:'Name two professionals who might be part of your maternity care team beyond your midwife.', a:'Any two: obstetrician, consultant, anaesthetist, health visitor, sonographer, perinatal mental health worker, doula, breastfeeding support worker, physiotherapist.', fac:'Many women don\'t know the full team available to them.' },
      { format:'True/False', diff:'Easy', q:'TRUE or FALSE: You can ask for a different midwife during labour if you feel uncomfortable.', a:'TRUE. You can request a change of midwife. While not always possible due to staffing, you are entitled to ask and your discomfort is a valid reason.', fac:'Many women don\'t know this. It can be life-changing in a difficult birth.' },
      { format:'Quiz', diff:'Medium', q:'What is a doula and is their support available on the NHS?', a:'A trained non-clinical birth companion providing emotional, physical and informational support before, during and after birth. Not routinely NHS-funded but some charities offer funded programmes.', fac:'Mention any local doula programmes relevant to Brixton/Peckham if known.' },
      { format:'Advocacy', diff:'Medium', q:'You are in labour and feel your concerns are being ignored. Your birth partner is with you. What can THEY do to advocate for you?', a:'Good responses: ask for a senior midwife, request a pause, repeat your stated preferences clearly, ask "what does my partner need right now?", document what\'s happening.', fac:'Pair exercise: one person plays patient, one the birth partner.' },
      { format:'Play-Doh', diff:'Medium', q:'Sculpt what it feels like to advocate for yourself in a clinical setting. 90 seconds. Share with the group.', a:'No correct answer. What did you make and what does it represent?', fac:'Watch for models representing fear, silence or being small — important to acknowledge.' },
      { format:'Dingbats', diff:'Easy', q:'🗣️ + 🏥 + 💪 = ?', a:'Speak Up in Hospital — using your voice to ensure your needs, preferences and concerns are heard and acted on in a clinical setting.', fac:'Ask: what stops women speaking up in hospitals? Collect answers.' },
      { format:'Quiz', diff:'Medium', q:'What is the main risk of uncontrolled hypertension in pregnancy?', a:'Pre-eclampsia — which can lead to eclampsia (seizures), HELLP syndrome, placental abruption, premature birth, and in severe cases maternal or fetal death.', fac:'Pre-eclampsia is closely linked to maternal death in the UK. Worth naming clearly.' },
      { format:'Quiz', diff:'Hard', q:'What does HbA1c measure and why is it relevant in gestational diabetes?', a:'HbA1c measures average blood sugar levels over 2–3 months. In gestational diabetes it helps assess how well blood sugar has been controlled and whether medication is needed.', fac:'The difference between HbA1c and a GTT (glucose tolerance test) is worth explaining.' },
      { format:'Quiz', diff:'Medium', q:'Name two symptoms of a sickle cell crisis in pregnancy.', a:'Any two: severe pain in bones, chest or abdomen, breathlessness, fever, swelling in hands or feet, extreme fatigue. Requires immediate hospital assessment.', fac:'Women with sickle cell disease need specialist consultant-led care throughout pregnancy.' },
      { format:'True/False', diff:'Medium', q:'TRUE or FALSE: Women with sickle cell trait (not disease) face the same pregnancy risks as women with sickle cell disease.', a:'FALSE. Sickle cell trait (carrying one gene) generally causes fewer complications than sickle cell disease (carrying two genes). However, trait carriers should still be monitored.', fac:'Important distinction — many women don\'t know which they have. Encourage testing.' },
      { format:'True/False', diff:'Easy', q:'TRUE or FALSE: Pre-eclampsia can develop after birth, not just during pregnancy.', a:'TRUE. Post-partum pre-eclampsia can develop up to 6 weeks after birth. Warning signs include severe headache, visual disturbances and upper abdominal pain.', fac:'Many women (and some clinicians) don\'t know this. It is a serious postnatal risk.' },
      { format:'Health Literacy', diff:'Hard', q:'"Your BP reading is 148/95 at 34 weeks." What does this mean and what should happen next?', a:'148/95 is above the threshold for gestational hypertension (140/90). You should be assessed for pre-eclampsia — urine dip, bloods, and fetal monitoring. This requires prompt clinical review.', fac:'Explain the difference between gestational hypertension and pre-eclampsia.' },
      { format:'Health Literacy', diff:'Hard', q:'"Your GTT result is 7.8 mmol/L at 2 hours." Is this within normal range?', a:'No. The NICE threshold for gestational diabetes at the 2-hour GTT reading is 7.8 mmol/L or above. This result meets the diagnostic criteria for gestational diabetes.', fac:'Key: at-the-threshold does not mean "borderline fine" — it meets the diagnostic criteria.' },
      { format:'Quiz', diff:'Medium', q:'What is Group B Strep (GBS) and how is it usually detected in pregnancy?', a:'GBS is a bacterium carried by around 1 in 4 women. Usually harmless to the mother but can cause serious infection in newborns. Detected via a vaginal/rectal swab — not routinely offered by the NHS but can be done privately.', fac:'GBS+ women are offered IV antibiotics in labour to protect the baby.' },
      { format:'True/False', diff:'Easy', q:'TRUE or FALSE: GBS always causes illness in newborns when the mother is a carrier.', a:'FALSE. Most babies born to GBS-positive mothers are unaffected. The risk is around 1 in 1,750 births. IV antibiotics in labour reduce this risk significantly.', fac:'Balance: don\'t cause unnecessary panic, but ensure women understand the importance of knowing their GBS status.' },
    ]
  },

